    <header class="cs-hero">
        <div class="cs-hero-inner">
            <nav class="cs-crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href='/'>Home</a> &rsaquo; <a href='/case-studies'>Case studies</a> &rsaquo; Rent vs buy decision model</nav>
            <div class="cs-eyebrow"><i class="fas fa-house-chimney"></i> Case study &middot; Simulation Â· Financial modelling</div>
            <h1>Is renting really &ldquo;dead money&rdquo;? A decision model, tested against 21 years of history</h1>
            <p class="cs-dek">We simulated renting and buying the same home, month by month, for 12 regions and 316 local authorities, then checked the model against what actually happened to buyers from 2005 to 2026.</p>
            <div class="cs-chips"><span class="cs-chip">Simulation</span><span class="cs-chip">Financial modelling</span><span class="cs-chip">Backtesting</span><span class="cs-chip">Sensitivity analysis</span><span class="cs-chip">Python and R</span></div>
            <div class="cs-hero-meta"><span><i class="fas fa-flask"></i>Independent project on public data</span><span><i class="fas fa-code"></i>Code and data published</span><span><i class="far fa-calendar"></i>Updated 1 November 2026</span></div>
        </div>
    </header>

    <section class="cs-metrics" aria-label="Key results">
            <div class="cs-metric"><div class="cs-metric-value">316</div><div class="cs-metric-label">local authorities and 12 regions modelled, month by month, over 30 years</div></div>
            <div class="cs-metric"><div class="cs-metric-value">1.4&ndash;4.1<small>yrs</small></div><div class="cs-metric-label">for buying to beat renting by region, if prices keep rising 3% a year</div></div>
            <div class="cs-metric is-warn"><div class="cs-metric-value">30+<small>yrs</small></div><div class="cs-metric-label">in five regions if house prices stay flat</div></div>
            <div class="cs-metric"><div class="cs-metric-value">120/120</div><div class="cs-metric-label">regional results reproduced to the month by an independent R implementation</div></div>
    </section>

    <main class="cs-layout">
        <aside class="cs-glance" aria-label="At a glance">
            <h2>At a glance</h2>
            <dl>
                    <dt>Question</dt><dd>When does buying a home leave you richer than renting the same home and investing the difference?</dd>
                    <dt>Data</dt><dd>Official house prices, private rents and mortgage rates, July 2005 to September 2026</dd>
                    <dt>Methods</dt><dd>Monthly cash-flow simulation, equal-spend comparison, historical backtest, sensitivity grids</dd>
                    <dt>Built with</dt><dd>Python, R, pandas, matplotlib, JavaScript</dd>
                    <dt>Deliverables</dt><dd>A long-form article, six charts, open data files and a <a href='/tools/rent-vs-buy-calculator'>rent vs buy calculator</a></dd>
            </dl>
            <a href="https://calendly.com/truthwithdata/30min" class="btn btn-primary cs-glance-cta" rel="noopener">Discuss a similar project</a>
            <nav class="cs-toc" aria-label="On this page">
                    <a href="#question">The question</a>
                    <a href="#approach">The approach</a>
                    <a href="#findings">What we found</a>
                    <a href="#business">Where this applies</a>
                    <a href="#limits">Limits</a>
                    <a href="#engagement">How we would do this for you</a>
                    <a href="#reproduce">Reproduce it</a>
            </nav>
        </aside>

        <article class="cs-body">

            <section class="cs-section" id="question">
                <h2><span class="cs-num">01</span>The question</h2>
                <p class="cs-lede">&ldquo;Renting is dead money&rdquo; is one of the most repeated pieces of financial advice in the world. It is half right: rent buys you nothing you can sell. But most early mortgage payments are interest, and buying costs, repairs and insurance are just as unrecoverable.</p>
                <p>The honest question is which set of unrecoverable costs is smaller, for the same home, over the time you will live in it. It is a decision worth hundreds of thousands, usually made on a rule of thumb. We used the UK as the test market because it publishes matched price and rent data for every local area. The same model works anywhere with house prices, rents and mortgage rates.</p>
            </section>

            <section class="cs-section" id="approach">
                <h2><span class="cs-num">02</span>The approach</h2>
                <ol class="cs-steps">
                    <li><h3>One home, two households</h3><p>One household buys the home. The other rents the same home and invests the deposit and buying costs. Each month, whoever pays less for housing invests the difference, so both spend exactly the same.</p></li>
                    <li><h3>Every cost, month by month</h3><p>Stamp duty, legal fees, survey, mortgage fees on every re-fix, maintenance, insurance, service charges, and the cost of selling at the end. Capital repayment counts as saving, not as a cost.</p></li>
                    <li><h3>A break-even that means something</h3><p>We report the month from which the buyer stays ahead for good, not the first time they pull ahead, so a brief lead before a re-fix fee or a price dip does not count.</p></li>
                    <li><h3>Backtest against history</h3><p>We replayed every purchase year from 2005 with the prices, rents and mortgage rates that actually followed, including the 2008 crash and the 2022 rate shock.</p></li>
                    <li><h3>Stress the assumptions</h3><p>Grids of mortgage rates (3&ndash;7%) against house-price growth (0&ndash;5%), deposits of 10% and 25%, and four property types.</p></li>
                    <li><h3>Check it twice, then ship it</h3><p>An independent R implementation reproduces all 120 region &times; property type &times; deposit results to the month. The calculator's JavaScript was checked against the same 120 cases.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </section>

            <section class="cs-section" id="findings">
                <h2><span class="cs-num">03</span>What we found</h2>
                <p>Mortgage interest alone is 77% to 107% of the rent on the same home in the first year. Add maintenance, insurance and the return the deposit could have earned, and owning costs 105% to 145% of the rent in every region. Whether buying wins depends almost entirely on what house prices do next.</p>
                <figure class="cs-figure cs-figure-scroll">
                    <img src="../blog/charts/rent-vs-buy-breakeven-years-by-region-uk-2026.svg" alt="Dot plot of break-even years by region under three scenarios: 1.4 to 4.1 years if prices keep rising 3% a year, 2.3 to 12.2 years if mortgage rates are a point higher, and 11 years to more than 30 if prices are flat." loading="lazy" width="893" height="533">
                    <figcaption><strong>Figure 1.</strong> Buying wins in 1.4 to 4.1 years if prices keep rising at their 2005&ndash;2026 pace. A one-point rise in mortgage rates stretches that to as much as 12 years; flat prices push five regions beyond 30.</figcaption>
                </figure>
                <figure class="cs-figure cs-figure-scroll">
                    <img width="893" height="576" src="../blog/charts/rent-vs-buy-historical-backtest-buy-year-years-held-2005-2026.svg" alt="Heatmap of buyer-minus-renter wealth by purchase year and years held, 2005 to 2026. Buyers in 2006 to 2008 were behind for up to a decade; buyers from 2009 to 2021 were mostly ahead after five years; buyers in 2022 to 2024 were behind after two years." loading="lazy">
                    <figcaption><strong>Figure 2.</strong> The backtest. Buyers were ahead in 47% of cases after one year, 79% after five and 86% after ten. People who bought at the 2007 peak took 6 to 19 years to get ahead, and 2022&ndash;24 buyers are behind in 8 of 12 regions.</figcaption>
                </figure>
                <figure class="cs-figure cs-figure-scroll">
                    <img width="763" height="518" src="../blog/charts/rent-vs-buy-breakeven-sensitivity-mortgage-rate-price-growth.svg" alt="Heatmap of median break-even years by mortgage rate and house-price growth. At today's rate it is 2.8 years with 3% price growth, 8.0 years with 2%, 19 years with 1% and 26 years with flat prices." loading="lazy">
                    <figcaption><strong>Figure 3.</strong> The answer turns on one uncertain input. At today's rates the median break-even is 2.8 years with 3% price growth, 8 years with 2% and 26 years with flat prices.</figcaption>
                </figure>
                <p class="cs-takeaway">The useful output is not a single answer but the conditions under which each answer holds, which is what a good decision model should give you.</p>
            </section>

            <section class="cs-section" id="business">
                <h2><span class="cs-num">04</span>Where this applies</h2>
                <div class="cs-value-grid">
                    <div class="cs-value"><i class="fas fa-building-columns"></i><h3>Lenders and brokers</h3><p>Affordability and break-even tools for customers that show the risks as well as the upside, with rates and prices you can update monthly.</p></div>
                    <div class="cs-value"><i class="fas fa-mobile-screen"></i><h3>Fintech and proptech</h3><p>A calculator engine checked against an independent implementation, ready to sit behind an app or a lead-generation page.</p></div>
                    <div class="cs-value"><i class="fas fa-user-tie"></i><h3>Advisers and HR teams</h3><p>Relocation, lease-or-buy and build-or-rent decisions use the same structure: two cash-flow paths, equal spend, every cost counted.</p></div>
                    <div class="cs-value"><i class="fas fa-chart-line"></i><h3>Any "now or later" decision</h3><p>Lease vs buy equipment, cloud vs on-premises, subscribe vs own. Backtest the rule of thumb before trusting it.</p></div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section class="cs-section" id="limits">
                <h2><span class="cs-num">05</span>Limits</h2>
                <div class="cs-callout is-limits">
                    <h3><i class="fas fa-triangle-exclamation"></i> What the model leaves out</h3>
                    <ul>
                        <li>The forward model holds the mortgage rate constant; the sensitivity grid shows how much that matters.</li>
                        <li>The renter earns a steady 5% a year. Real investment returns are lumpy, and that assumption probably flatters buyers in 2009&ndash;2021.</li>
                        <li>Today's stamp duty rules are applied to past purchases in the backtest, which understates tax before 2014.</li>
                        <li>It compares the average home in an area; any one home and household will differ.</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section class="cs-section" id="engagement">
                <h2><span class="cs-num">06</span>How we would do this for you</h2>
                <div class="cs-engage">
                    <div class="cs-engage-step"><div class="cs-when">Week 1</div><h3>Map the decision</h3><p>Every cash flow, assumption and data source written down and agreed before any code is written.</p></div>
                    <div class="cs-engage-step"><div class="cs-when">Weeks 2&ndash;3</div><h3>Model, backtest, stress</h3><p>The simulation, a backtest on your history, and sensitivity grids for the inputs nobody can forecast.</p></div>
                    <div class="cs-engage-step"><div class="cs-when">Week 4</div><h3>Ship the tool</h3><p>A calculator or dashboard verified against the model, plus a monthly data-refresh script.</p></div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section class="cs-section" id="reproduce">
                <h2><span class="cs-num">07</span>Reproduce it</h2>
                <div class="cs-stack"><span>Python 3</span><span>R</span><span>pandas</span><span>matplotlib</span><span>JavaScript</span><span>Official open data</span></div>
                <div class="cs-links">
                    <a class='btn btn-primary' href='/tools/rent-vs-buy-calculator'><i class="fas fa-calculator"></i>Try the calculator</a>
                    <a class='btn btn-secondary' href='/blog/is-renting-dead-money-uk'><i class="fas fa-newspaper"></i>Read the full analysis</a>
                    <a class="btn btn-outline" href="https://github.com/truthwithdata/analysis/tree/main/rent-vs-buy-uk" rel="noopener"><i class="fab fa-github"></i>Code on GitHub</a>
                </div>
                <p style="margin-top:18px;font-size:0.95rem;color:#64748b">Data: HM Land Registry UK House Price Index; ONS Price Index of Private Rents; Bank of England quoted mortgage rates.</p>
            </section>

            <p class="cs-disclosure"><i class="fas fa-circle-info"></i><span>This is an independent project built on public data to show how we work. It is not client work, and no client data was used. Figures are reproducible from the published code.</span></p>
        </article>
    </main>

